{"kind":"report","text":"Review of task/resident-append at 9c4f591545ee54e1877ab8cce0d3579a15226ab7: APPROVED at this exact head. Head verified exact, worktree clean before and after my probes, scope is 234 lines across the kernel, its tests, and the app wiring. make vet, make test, make build, and go test -race ./... all pass. Cache invalidation rests on one conjunction — the cached target must equal this target and the cached head must equal the head just re-read from the ref — and the head is re-read at the top of every retry attempt, which is what makes the individual conditions fall out. Cold start fails the predicate on an empty cache; an external advance fails it because the re-read head has moved, and this is covered twice, once in the kernel test and once in the app test where a write through one Workspace is observed through another sharing the same common directory; CAS loss continues the loop and so re-reads the head, which the second kernel test exercises; and the stateless path passes a nil cache, so Submit can never consult one. Target change is guarded by the same conjunction as head change, and I want to be exact about what I did not do: I did not construct two targets in one store to exercise that limb, so I am reasoning from the shared predicate rather than reporting an observation. Successful CAS updates dedup and frontier correctly, and the branch's test does not take its own word for it — it cross-checks against an independent full Verify, requiring the verified head to equal the cached head and the event count to match. The caller-owned bytes condition is the one I looked at hardest, because it is where a cache stops being an optimisation and becomes a trust question. cloneSigned deep-copies Intent, ActorKey, and Signature, and I confirmed those are exactly the three byte-slice fields Signed has, so nothing caller-owned is retained by reference there. The decoded Intent, however, is cached without cloning and carries a CapabilityHash []byte, which would alias caller memory if the decoder returned a window into its input. I checked rather than assumed, and checked twice: a stock decoder said no, but the project pins a different cbor version behind a custom DecMode, so I ran a temporary probe inside this branch through the project's own intent.Decode with its real options, mutating the source buffer afterwards, and neither CapabilityHash nor the string fields moved. The branch's own test attacks the same surface from the other side by flipping a bit in the caller's Intent after submission and requiring the later replay to still succeed. Serialization does not break concurrency semantics, and since no existing test covers contention I wrote one: thirty-six concurrent submissions through a single Submitter, twelve distinct requests with three duplicates each, run under the race detector. Every submission succeeded, all duplicates of a request resolved to the same single commit, exactly twelve distinct commits landed, an independent Verify accepted the log, the cached head agreed with the verified head, and a genuine idempotency conflict was still refused against the warm cache. Bounded warm cost is supported, but the benchmark only demonstrates it when run long enough, and I nearly misread it. At one iteration the resident path is 117.6 ms against the stateless 115.3 ms — no benefit at all, because a single submission can never hit a cache it has just populated. At ten appends the figures are 342.1 ms and 75.7 ms per operation; at forty they are 1,118.2 ms and 73.1 ms. The claim is in the shape rather than the ratio: the resident per-append cost is flat as the log grows, 75.7 to 73.1 ms and 874 to 841 allocations, while the stateless cost rises with depth, 342 to 1,118 ms and 3,675 to 11,703 allocations. A reviewer running the default would see the first pair and conclude wrongly, so I record it here. Two non-blocking observations. The Submitter mutex is held across the whole submission, including git subprocess calls and the PreAppend hook, so resident throughput is serialized and a slow hook stalls every caller; that is a deliberate trade for a correctness-critical cache and it is correct as written, but it is a ceiling worth knowing, and a PreAppend that ever re-entered Submit would deadlock, which no caller does today. Second, the cache deliberately drops log.Events before storing, keeping the verified frontier and dedup projection without a second resident copy of the stream; I confirmed submit never reads that field, so the omission is safe rather than lucky. Both temporary probes were removed and the worktree verifies clean at the reviewed head. Approval is for 9c4f591545ee54e1877ab8cce0d3579a15226ab7 only, any new commit requires a new review, and I have not merged anything.","body":{"benchmark":"resident per-op flat 75.7ms/874 allocs at 10 appends to 73.1ms/841 at 40; stateless 342.1ms/3675 to 1118.2ms/11703; at 1 iteration the two are indistinguishable","gates":"make vet, make test, make build, go test -race ./... all pass; head exact; worktree clean before and after probes","head":"9c4f591545ee54e1877ab8cce0d3579a15226ab7","independent_verification":"probed the project's real cbor DecMode for aliasing of Intent.CapabilityHash (none); wrote and ran a 36-way concurrent submission test under -race (all landed, duplicates shared one commit, independent Verify agreed, conflict still refused)","result":"approved"}}